<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<section xml:id="sec-spanning-trees">
  <title>Spanning Trees</title>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Outcomes</title>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>Recognize trees and spanning trees and use their basic properties.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Apply Kruskal's algorithm to find a minimum-cost spanning tree.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </objectives>
  
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-st-vocabulary">
    <title>Connecting a network</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-st-tree">
      <title>Tree</title>
      <statement>
        <p> </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="def-st-spanning-tree">
      <title>Spanning Tree</title>
      <statement>
        <p> </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      <em>Properties of a tree</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>A tree with <m>n</m> vertices has exactly <m>n-1</m> edges.</li>
        <li>There is exactly one simple path between any two vertices.</li>
        <li>Removing any edge disconnects the tree.</li>
        <li>Adding an edge between two of its vertices creates a circuit.</li>
        <li>Vertices may have degree greater than 2.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-st-first-tree">
      <title>Keep the buildings connected</title>
      <statement>
        
        <image source="images/st-original.png" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>Undirected graph. Vertices: A, B, C, D, E, F, G. Edges: AB; BC; CD; AE; BE; BF; CF; CG; DG; EF; FG. There are no other edges. Crossings without a marked vertex are not vertices.</shortdescription>
        </image>
          
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>How many edges must a spanning tree have? Select one spanning tree by marking the edges to keep.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-st-recognize-and-kruskal">
    <title>Recognizing and choosing a spanning tree</title>
    
    <example xml:id="ex-st-candidates">
      <title>Compare three candidates</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Each candidate uses only connections from the seven-building graph. Candidate III omits building G.</p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Candidate I</caption>
          <image source="images/st-candidate-i.png" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>Undirected graph. Vertices: A, B, C, D, E, F, G. Edges: AB; BC; CD; BE; EF; FG. There are no other edges. Crossings without a marked vertex are not vertices.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <caption>Candidate II</caption>
          <image source="images/st-candidate-ii.png" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>Undirected graph. Vertices: A, B, C, D, E, F, G. Edges: AB; AE; BE; CF; FG; DG. There are no other edges. Crossings without a marked vertex are not vertices.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <caption>Candidate III (G is omitted)</caption>
          <image source="images/st-candidate-iii.png" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>Undirected graph. Vertices: A, B, C, D, E, F. Edges: AB; BC; CD; BE; EF. There are no other edges. Crossings without a marked vertex are not vertices.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <tabular halign="left" valign="middle" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="62.500%" right="minor"/>
          <col width="12.500%" right="minor"/>
          <col width="12.500%" right="minor"/>
          <col width="12.500%" right="minor"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Check</cell>
            <cell>I</cell>
            <cell>II</cell>
            <cell>III</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Contains every original vertex?</cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Connected?</cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Contains a circuit?</cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Is a spanning tree of the original graph?</cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
            <cell>
              <fillin characters="9"/>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-st-minimum-cost">
      <title>Minimum-Cost Spanning Tree (MCST)</title>
      <statement>
        <p> </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <algorithm xml:id="alg-st-kruskal">
      <title>Kruskal's algorithm</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>Select the cheapest unused edge in the graph.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Repeat step 1, adding the cheapest unused edge, unless adding the edge would create a circuit
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Repeat until a spanning tree is formed</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>

    <remark>
      <p>The selected edges need not be connected at intermediate steps. Equal-weight edges may be considered in either order. If the original graph is disconnected, no spanning tree exists.</p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-st-kruskal-example">
    <title>Practice</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-st-campus-cable">
      <title>Cable installation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The graph shows fourteen available connections among eight buildings. The weights are installation costs, in thousands of dollars. Use Kruskal’s algorithm to find a minimum-cost spanning tree.</p>
        
        <image source="images/st-kruskal.png" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>Undirected graph. Vertices: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H. Edges and weights: AB: 2; BE: 3; AE: 4; BC: 5; EF: 6; BF: 7; CF: 8; CD: 9; FG: 10; CG: 11; DG: 12; EH: 13; GH: 14; AH: 15. There are no other edges. Crossings without a marked vertex are not vertices. Weights are thousands of dollars.</shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>Number of vertices: ________     Required number of selected edges: ________</p>
        
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>List the selected edges and report the total installation cost in dollars.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Trace the selected edges on the graph. Which vertices have degree 3, and why is that allowed?</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Explain why AC is not an eligible edge in this problem, even if it looks like a useful connection.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Identify the first rejected edge and the circuit it would create.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    
